Add the hardware dim command (0x1C), which this library never implemented.

The controller dims in hardware. docs/Knowledgebase/old_afxcontroller-protocoll-
documentaion.txt in this very repository documents it:

    ### 0x1C: Dim
        02:1C:oo:bb:  :  :  :  :
        o: 32 (Enable)
           64 (Disable)
        b: 01 (Always)
           00 (in Battery Mode Only)

but cmdpacket.py's command table stops at CMD_SET_SPEED = 0xe, so nothing could
send it. Confirmed on an Alienware 14 (0x187c:0x0525, controller_m14xr3, 4 bits
per colour): with the controller reset and settled first, 02:1C:32:01 answers
STATUS_READY -- while a deliberately bogus command in the same run answers
STATUS_UNKNOWN_COMMAND -- and on a keyboard painted pure white, toggling Enable
and Disable visibly dims and restores it.

It matters because dimming by scaling the RGB values cannot work here: with 16
levels per channel, a colour whose smallest lit channel is a small fraction of
its largest loses that channel first, so the hue shifts as the lights dim. A
state-based hardware dim leaves the colours exactly as sent.

Dimming is deliberately not part of the --theme/--list dispatch chain, so
`--theme X --dim on` does both in one run and one device claim.

diff -ru a/alienfx/core/cmdpacket.py b/alienfx/core/cmdpacket.py
--- a/alienfx/core/cmdpacket.py	2026-09-27 03:06:10.809061597 -0500
+++ b/alienfx/core/cmdpacket.py	2026-09-27 03:06:10.844185712 -0500
@@ -57,6 +57,13 @@
     CMD_SAVE_NEXT = 0x8
     CMD_SAVE = 0x9
     CMD_SET_SPEED = 0xe
+    CMD_SET_DIM = 0x1c
+
+    # 0x1C takes a state, not a level: the controller dims by a fixed amount or not
+    # at all. Values from docs/Knowledgebase/old_afxcontroller-protocoll-documentaion.txt
+    # and confirmed on an Alienware 14 (0x187c:0x0525).
+    DIM_ENABLE = 0x32
+    DIM_DISABLE = 0x64
 
     # Status codes
     STATUS_BUSY = 0x11
@@ -92,7 +99,8 @@
             self.CMD_RESET: self._parse_cmd_reset,
             self.CMD_SAVE_NEXT: self._parse_cmd_save_next,
             self.CMD_SAVE: self._parse_cmd_save,
-            self.CMD_SET_SPEED: self._parse_cmd_set_speed
+            self.CMD_SET_SPEED: self._parse_cmd_set_speed,
+            self.CMD_SET_DIM: self._parse_cmd_set_dim
         }
 
     # @staticmethod
@@ -277,6 +285,15 @@
         pkt = args["pkt"]
         return "SET_SPEED: {}".format(hex((pkt[2] << 8) + pkt[3]))
 
+    def _parse_cmd_set_dim(self, args):
+        """ Parse a packet containing the "dim" command and return it as a
+        human readable string.
+        """
+        pkt = args["pkt"]
+        state = "ENABLE" if pkt[2] == self.DIM_ENABLE else "DISABLE"
+        return "SET_DIM: {} ({})".format(
+            state, "always" if pkt[3] else "battery-only")
+
     # @classmethod
     def _parse_cmd_unknown(self, args):
         """ Return a string reporting an unknown command packet.
@@ -388,3 +405,15 @@
         pkt = [0x02, self.CMD_SET_SPEED, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]
         pkt[2:4] = [(speed&0xff00) >> 8, speed & 0xff]
         return pkt
+
+    def make_cmd_set_dim(self, enable, always=True):
+        """ Return a command packet for the "dim" command.
+
+        Dimming is a state and not a level, so the colour is never scaled to
+        make the lights darker -- which on a 4-bit-per-channel controller
+        cannot be done without shifting the hue.
+        """
+        pkt = [0x02, self.CMD_SET_DIM, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]
+        pkt[2] = self.DIM_ENABLE if enable else self.DIM_DISABLE
+        pkt[3] = 0x01 if always else 0x00
+        return pkt
diff -ru a/alienfx/core/controller.py b/alienfx/core/controller.py
--- a/alienfx/core/controller.py	2026-09-27 03:06:10.809119804 -0500
+++ b/alienfx/core/controller.py	2026-09-27 03:06:10.844291788 -0500
@@ -268,6 +268,19 @@
             logging.debug("SENDING: {}".format(self.pkt_to_string(cmd)))
             self._driver.write_packet(cmd)
 
+    def set_dim(self, enable):
+        """ Enable or disable the controller's hardware dimming.
+
+        Independent of the theme: the colours already sent keep their exact
+        values, so this changes brightness without touching hue.
+        """
+        try:
+            self._driver.acquire()
+            self._wait_controller_ready()
+            self._send_cmds([self.cmd_packet.make_cmd_set_dim(enable)])
+        finally:
+            self._driver.release()
+
     def set_theme(self, themefile):
         """ Send the given theme settings to the controller. This should result
         in the lights changing to the theme settings immediately.
